i='TCS-3120';_,c=card(i);refs=c['references'];refs[0]['locator']='Section 2, p. 18:2 (finite vocabularies, Σₙ/Πₙ and substructures); Section 5, pp. 18:12–18:13 (even-level questions and known neighboring cases).'
refs.extend([dict(id='authorversion',title='Extension Preservation in the Finite and Prefix Classes of First Order Logic — author version',authors='Anuj Dawar; Abhisekh Sankaran',year=2020,url='https://arxiv.org/abs/2007.05459',pdf_url='https://abhisekhs.github.io/Papers/ext-pres.pdf',locator='ArXiv version 2, 24 October 2020; author PDF Section 5 retains both even-level questions. Version history checked 12 September 2026.'),dict(id='semiring2026',title='Preservation Theorems in Semiring Semantics',authors='Sophie Brinke; Anuj Dawar; Erich Grädel; Benedikt Pago',year=2026,url='https://doi.org/10.4230/LIPIcs.ICALP.2026.172',locator='Introduction and statement of finite-semiring preservation results. These concern non-Boolean interpretations and do not settle the classical Boolean even-prefix question.')])
save(i,dict(title='Do extension-closed even existential prefix classes collapse over finite structures?',formal='For every even integer k≥4, every finite relational vocabulary τ (allowing constants), and every first-order τ-sentence φ in Σ_k whose finite models are closed under extensions, is there a τ-sentence ψ in Π_k equivalent to φ on all finite τ-structures? Determine also the stronger version posed in the same source passage: can ψ always be chosen in Σ_{k−1}? The quantifier order is ∀k∀τ∀φ∃ψ. The sentence ψ may depend on k,τ,φ but not on the structure being tested. The two target fragments must be distinguished; failure or validity of one should not be silently attributed to the other.',definitions='A finite relational vocabulary τ consists of finitely many relation symbols of specified finite arities and finitely many constant symbols, with no positive-arity function symbols. A finite τ-structure A has a finite nonempty domain, interprets each r-ary relation as a subset of A^r, and interprets each constant as an element of A. Equality has its usual meaning. First-order formulas use these atomic predicates and equality, Boolean connectives, and quantifiers ∃x,∀x over the domain; a sentence has no free variables. A formula is in prenex form if a sequence of quantifiers precedes a quantifier-free matrix. Σ_j contains sentences with at most j consecutive blocks of like quantifiers, starting existentially; Π_j is the corresponding universal-starting class. Lower fragments and quantifier-free sentences are included using vacuous padding, valid for nonempty domains. Block lengths are arbitrary finite numbers; k bounds the number of blocks, not the total number of quantifiers. A⊆B means A’s domain is a subset of B’s, every constant has the same interpretation, and each relation of A is exactly the restriction of B’s relation to tuples in A. Thus an extension may add vertices and relations involving new vertices, but cannot change atomic facts on old vertices. The finite models of φ are extension-closed if A⊆B and A⊨φ imply B⊨φ for all finite τ-structures A,B. Equivalence in the finite means A⊨φ iff A⊨ψ for every finite τ-structure A; no equivalence on infinite structures is required. The vocabulary stays fixed when replacing φ by ψ, so no new predicates, constants or built-in order may be added. The question asserts existence of an equivalent sentence, not a uniform translation-time or sentence-length bound.',question_type='yes_no',answer_criterion='Settle the Π_k-collapse assertion and its stronger Σ_{k−1}-collapse version under the stated quantifiers. A positive answer requires a same-vocabulary equivalent sentence for every eligible φ. Refutation of either assertion requires an even k≥4 and a finite vocabulary and extension-closed Σ_k sentence having no equivalent sentence in that target fragment over finite structures. A separation at odd k or one that changes the vocabulary does not settle these assertions.',context_blocks=[dict(title='A semantic restriction may reduce quantifier alternation',text='Extension closure says that truth survives adding new elements while preserving the induced structure on old ones. The question asks whether this semantic restriction lets a sentence that starts with existential quantifiers be expressed in a simpler prefix class.',citation='primary'),dict(title='The unrestricted classical theorem fails on finite structures',text='When all structures are allowed, extension preservation characterizes existential sentences. Restricting preservation and equivalence to finite structures invalidates that characterization. The source strengthens this failure by showing that no fixed alternation bound captures every first-order extension-closed finite property.',citation='primary'),dict(title='Even and odd levels behave differently in known results',text='Every extension-closed Σ₂ property is already definable in Σ₁. By contrast, for every odd k>1 the source gives an extension-closed Σ_k property not definable in Π_k. Its even-k>2 question therefore remains after the broad hierarchy separation.',citation='primary'),dict(title='The two proposed collapses have different strength',text='A Σ_{k−1} sentence can be viewed as a Π_k sentence by adding a vacuous initial universal block. Thus the stronger requested collapse would imply the first one. The reverse implication is not supplied by this syntactic inclusion.',citation='primary'),dict(title='Recent preservation theorems use different truth values',text='The 2026 semiring work explicitly contrasts its finite preservation results with the failure in ordinary Boolean semantics and cites the original hierarchy separation. Its non-Boolean results do not provide the missing collapse or separation in this card.',citation='semiring2026')],why='Would identify the remaining parity-sensitive boundary between semantic preservation and quantifier alternation over finite structures, sharpening the expressive-power picture relevant to finite-model theory and database queries.',references=refs,progress=[dict(date='2021',text='The source proves odd-level separations, recalls the Σ₂-to-Σ₁ collapse and leaves both even-level targets open.',citation='primary'),dict(date='2026',text='Later semiring preservation work treats a different semantic setting and recalls the Boolean hierarchy separation; it does not state a solution to these even-level questions.',citation='semiring2026')],status='source_open',status_note='Checked the complete CSL paper, its author-version conclusion and arXiv history, and the introduction and stated finite-semiring results of the 2026 follow-up. Searches through 12 September 2026 found no resolution of either even-level Boolean target; status checking is not exhaustive.',model_self_contained=True,requires_context=False,importance=dict(score=c['importance']['score'],method='editorial',reason='A structural test of how extension preservation interacts with finite quantifier hierarchies, where neighboring levels show sharply different behavior. Existing score retained.',assessed_on=DATE),review_note='Recovered both nested even-level targets and specified finite structures, induced extensions, constants, same-vocabulary equivalence and block-count quantifiers. Separated odd-level and non-Boolean theorems and avoided adding a syntactic translation-time demand.',working_summary=dict(sentences=['The property must be definable by a first-order sentence and survive every finite extension.','For each even k at least four, the sentence initially lies in the existential-starting class Σ_k.','The question asks for equivalent universal-starting Π_k sentences, and more strongly for Σ_{k−1} sentences.','The source proves separations at odd levels while the second existential level already collapses.','Equivalence is over all finite structures in the same vocabulary, with no algorithmic translation bound.'],basis='saved_sources',source_basis='source-text',written_on=DATE,source_refs=['primary','authorversion','semiring2026'])),notes=['Restored the complete Π_k and stronger Σ_{k−1} questions for even k≥4.','Defined finite structures, induced extensions, same-vocabulary equivalence and quantifier blocks.','Separated semantic existence from an unrequested translation algorithm.','Checked the author version and recent semiring results without conflating their models.'],checked_sources=[r['url'] for r in refs]+['https://abhisekhs.github.io/Papers/ext-pres.pdf'])
